-- Q2491: Agency and Regulation [medium/L1]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under the Restatement (Third) of Agency, Section 8.14, a principal owes the agent a duty to compensate as agreed and reimburse authorized expenses incurred while performing agency duties. Abbott traveled to client sites as instructed and submitted documented receipts totaling $3,200, entitling him to reimbursement.
Wrong (A): No law requires a written employment contract before an agency relationship begins. Agency relationships may be created orally or by conduct.
Wrong (B): A principal does not owe a duty to ensure the agent''s physical safety at all times. The duty of indemnification is limited to authorized activities, not a blanket safety guarantee.
Wrong (C): A principal has no obligation to disclose complete financial records to the agent. The duty of disclosure is limited to information the agent needs to perform authorized tasks.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2491;

-- Q2492: Agency and Regulation [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under the Restatement (Third) of Agency, Section 8.09, an agent has a duty of obedience to act within the scope of authority granted by the principal. When an agent exceeds that authority and causes the principal to suffer a loss, the agent is personally liable for resulting damages.
Wrong (B): The principal does not assume the risk of unauthorized acts. An agent who acts outside the scope of authority bears responsibility for resulting losses.
Wrong (C): Liability does not depend on whether the agent profited. The agent is liable for losses caused by exceeding authority regardless of personal gain.
Wrong (D): The business judgment rule is a corporate law doctrine that protects directors making informed decisions. It does not apply to agents acting outside their authorized scope.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2492;

-- Q2493: Agency and Regulation [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under the Restatement (Third) of Agency, Section 3.07(1), the death of either the principal or the agent automatically terminates the agency relationship by operation of law. The agency is personal in nature and cannot survive the death of either party.
Wrong (A): Termination is not limited to mutual written agreement. Either party may unilaterally revoke or renounce the agency, and operation of law events also terminate it.
Wrong (B): A court order from a regulatory agency is not a standard method of terminating an agency relationship. Termination occurs by the parties'' acts or by operation of law.
Wrong (D): An oral agreement with no fixed term has no specified time period to expire. The agency continues until terminated by the parties or by operation of law.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2493;

-- Q2495: Agency and Regulation [medium/L1]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under the Restatement (Third) of Agency, Section 4.01, ratification requires that the principal had knowledge of all material facts and the capacity to have authorized the act originally. Mercer''s CEO affirmed Dawson''s unauthorized contract after learning the favorable terms, satisfying both requirements.
Wrong (B): Ratification does not require the third party''s express agreement or a signed writing. The principal''s unilateral affirmation is sufficient to bind the transaction.
Wrong (C): The agent''s bad faith is not a requirement for ratification. Ratification depends on the principal''s knowledge and capacity, not the agent''s intent when exceeding authority.
Wrong (D): No court filing is required. Ratification may occur through express statement, accepting benefits, or other conduct manifesting the principal''s assent.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2495;

-- Q2496: Agency and Regulation [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under the Restatement (Third) of Agency, Section 4.01, ratification occurs when a principal affirms a previously unauthorized act by accepting its benefits. By shipping the goods at the contract price, Wilson manifested assent to Martinez''s unauthorized transaction, binding Wilson to the terms.
Wrong (A): Wilson did not breach the agency agreement by shipping the goods. Rather, Wilson ratified Martinez''s act, affirming the unauthorized contract retroactively.
Wrong (B): Although Martinez exceeded authority, Wilson''s acceptance of the benefits waived any defense based on lack of authority. Ratification makes the act binding.
Wrong (D): Shipping goods to an existing customer does not create a new agency relationship. Wilson merely ratified the unauthorized sale through conduct.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2496;

-- Q2498: Agency and Regulation [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under the Restatement (Third) of Agency, Section 8.09, the duty of obedience requires an agent to follow the principal''s reasonable and lawful instructions. By selling the property for $180,000 despite a $200,000 minimum directive, the agent violated this express instruction regardless of personal market opinions.
Wrong (A): The duty of loyalty prohibits self-dealing and conflicts of interest. The agent did not personally profit or act disloyally; the agent simply disregarded specific pricing instructions.
Wrong (B): The duty of care concerns the agent''s competence and diligence in performing tasks. The issue here is not incompetence but direct violation of an express directive.
Wrong (C): The duty of notification requires communicating material facts to the principal. The agent''s failure was in disobeying the price floor, not in withholding information.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2498;

-- Q2500: Agency and Regulation [hard/L1]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under the Restatement (Third) of Agency, Section 3.12, an agency coupled with an interest exists when the agent holds a security interest in the subject matter. Because the bank''s authority to sell the warehouse secures its $500,000 loan, the principal cannot unilaterally revoke this authority.
Wrong (A): An agency coupled with an interest cannot be freely revoked. The agent''s property interest protects the authority from unilateral termination by the principal.
Wrong (C): Unlike an ordinary agency, an agency coupled with an interest does not terminate upon the principal''s death or incapacity. The agent''s independent stake survives these events.
Wrong (D): An agency coupled with an interest arises by agreement between the parties, not by court order. No judicial determination is required for its creation.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2500;

-- Q2502: Agency and Regulation [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under the Restatement (Third) of Agency, Section 8.11, an agent has a duty to use reasonable effort to provide the principal with facts that the agent knows or should know are material to the principal''s interests. A third party''s willingness to pay more is clearly material and must be promptly disclosed.
Wrong (B): Keeping material information confidential to negotiate a better commission violates the duty of loyalty. The agent must prioritize the principal''s interests over personal gain.
Wrong (C): Selling at the asking price without disclosing the higher offer fails the duty of notification. The agent must inform the principal before completing the transaction.
Wrong (D): There is no duty to report a willing buyer to a regulatory authority. The obligation runs to the principal, not to government agencies.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2502;

-- Q2505: Agency and Regulation [easy/L2]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under the Restatement (Third) of Agency, Section 1.04, a special agent is authorized to conduct a single transaction or a limited series of transactions. The broker''s authority is confined to finding a buyer and selling one specific property, making the broker a special agent.
Wrong (B): A general agent has broad, continuing authority to conduct a range of business activities. A broker authorized for only one sale does not have continuing authority.
Wrong (C): An undisclosed agent acts for a principal whose existence is unknown to the third party. The facts do not indicate the seller''s existence is hidden from the buyer.
Wrong (D): A gratuitous agent acts without compensation. Real estate brokers typically earn a commission, so this classification does not apply.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2505;

-- Q2506: Business Structures [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under IRC Sec. 1, a sole proprietorship is not a separate taxable entity. All business income and expenses are reported on Schedule C of Form 1040 as part of the owner''s individual return. Helen''s $68,000 revenue and $22,000 expenses flow through Schedule C.
Wrong (A): Schedule E reports rental, royalty, partnership, and S corporation income. Sole proprietor business income belongs on Schedule C, not Schedule E.
Wrong (B): Form 1120 is the corporate income tax return filed by C corporations under IRC Sec. 11. A sole proprietorship is not a corporation and does not file Form 1120.
Wrong (C): Form 1065 is the partnership information return filed under IRC Sec. 701. A sole proprietorship is not a partnership and has no separate entity-level filing requirement.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2506;

-- Q2507: Business Structures [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under IRC Sec. 1401, the self-employment tax rate is 15.3%, consisting of 12.4% for Social Security (OASDI) and 2.9% for Medicare (HI), applied to 92.35% of net self-employment earnings per IRC Sec. 1402(a). Self-employed individuals bear both the employer and employee shares.
Wrong (A): The 7.65% rate represents only the employee''s share of FICA (6.2% Social Security + 1.45% Medicare). Self-employed individuals must pay both halves, totaling 15.3%.
Wrong (B): The 12.4% rate covers only the Social Security component. It omits the 2.9% Medicare portion, which brings the total self-employment tax rate to 15.3%.
Wrong (D): The 6.2% rate is the employee-only Social Security tax. Self-employed taxpayers owe the combined employer and employee rate of 15.3%.$EXPL$
