-- Q2509: Business Structures [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under RUPA Section 306(a), each partner in a general partnership has joint and several liability for all partnership obligations. The supplier can collect the entire $250,000 remaining debt ($300,000 minus $50,000 partnership assets) from Tom personally, regardless of his proportionate share. Tom would have a right of contribution against Jerry.
Wrong (A): Tom''s liability is not limited to his $100,000 capital contribution. In a general partnership, personal assets are fully exposed beyond any capital invested.
Wrong (B): The debt is not split in half. Joint and several liability means the supplier can pursue the full remaining amount from any single partner.
Wrong (D): This calculation incorrectly offsets Jerry''s share. The supplier is not required to collect proportionally and may recover the entire remaining debt from Tom alone.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2509;

-- Q2510: Business Structures [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under RULPA Section 303, a limited partner''s liability is generally limited to their capital contribution. Morgan''s maximum exposure is the $150,000 invested, not the full $400,000 in partnership debts. This protection holds so long as the limited partner does not exercise control over the business.
Wrong (A): A limited partner is not personally liable for all partnership debts. That level of exposure applies only to general partners under RUPA Section 306(a).
Wrong (B): A limited partner is liable up to the amount of their capital contribution. Stating they are never liable for any amount is incorrect.
Wrong (C): Joint and several liability applies to general partners, not limited partners. Limited partners do not share the general partner''s unlimited personal exposure.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2510;

-- Q2511: Business Structures [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under RULPA Section 403, general partners bear unlimited personal liability for all partnership debts, while limited partners risk only their capital contributions. The two general partners are personally liable for the full $500,000 and can be pursued individually for the entire amount.
Wrong (A): Liability is not shared equally among all twelve partners. Limited partners'' exposure is capped at their invested capital, while general partners bear unlimited liability.
Wrong (C): A limited partner''s voting on a particular transaction does not trigger personal liability for that debt. Liability exposure depends on the partner''s classification, not individual votes.
Wrong (D): A limited partnership still has partners with personal liability. The general partners remain fully exposed for all partnership obligations.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2511;

-- Q2512: Business Structures [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under RULLCA Section 304, LLC members receive limited liability protection while retaining the right to actively participate in management. This is the key structural advantage over a general partnership, where active management participation exposes partners to unlimited personal liability.
Wrong (A): This statement is false. LLC members can participate in management under RULLCA without losing their liability protection, unlike limited partners under RULPA.
Wrong (B): Most states allow single-member LLCs. There is no requirement that an LLC have at least two members under RULLCA.
Wrong (C): LLCs are not always taxed as corporations. By default, a multi-member LLC is taxed as a partnership, and a single-member LLC is disregarded for federal tax purposes.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2512;

-- Q2514: Business Structures [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under RULLCA Section 503, a creditor of an individual LLC member is limited to obtaining a charging order against the member''s transferable interest. The charging order entitles the creditor to receive distributions that would otherwise go to the debtor-member but does not allow seizure of assets or forced dissolution.
Wrong (A): A personal judgment creditor of a member cannot collect directly from LLC assets. The LLC is a separate legal entity, and its assets are not subject to a member''s personal debts.
Wrong (B): The creditor cannot seize and sell the membership interest itself. The exclusive remedy under most statutes is a charging order on distributions, not a forced sale.
Wrong (C): A personal creditor of one member cannot compel dissolution of the entire LLC. The charging order protects the other members from disruption caused by one member''s debts.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2514;

